# Fixture for x64_bigint_semantics.feature (GitHub #188): a bare integer
# LITERAL immediately followed by `.member` (no space, no parens around the
# literal). The self-hosted lexer used to consume the `.` into the number
# text itself (treating it as the start of a float literal, since digit-dot
# scanning never checked whether a digit actually follows), leaving a
# malformed NUM token and no `.` token for the parser to see at all -- a
# parse error, not a runtime issue. Run under `pat --ir-run` and compiled
# with `patc1 --x64`; the outputs must match.
#
# `2.5.to_s` is deliberately NOT covered here: it exposes a separate,
# unrelated native-x64 gap (a genuine unboxed float value is
# indistinguishable from a plain int to any generic function -- type_of,
# get, `.to_s` -- unless the calling context happens to be dynamic-mode and
# actually boxes it; filed separately).
print(233.to_s)
print(4.to_s)
print([1, 2, 3].length)
let n = 5
print(n.to_s)
print("after")
